# sharkd's global preferences, read from the data directory of the Wireshark
# installed next to it - so every program in this image starts with these columns.
#
# The only reason the file exists: Source and Destination carry addresses but not
# ports, and the sequence-diagram view wants both, as Wireshark's own flow graph
# labels each arrow end with its port. sharkd will not add a column on request -
# `frames` takes no column list, and `setconf gui.column.format` after startup
# renames the columns without changing what they hold - so the ports have to be
# columns from the very start.
#
# They are hidden columns: `status` reports them "visible": false, the packet list
# skips them, and the flow view reads the ports out of the rows it already has.
# Everything else below is Wireshark's default column set, unchanged.

gui.column.format:
	"No.", "%m",
	"Time", "%t",
	"Source", "%s",
	"SrcPort", "%uS",
	"Destination", "%d",
	"DstPort", "%uD",
	"Protocol", "%p",
	"Length", "%L",
	"Info", "%i"

# 1-indexed into the list above, hence the two port columns. The format-based
# spelling of this preference (gui.column.hidden) is deprecated in master.
gui.column.hide: 4,6

# ESP, because Gm is protected with it: 3GPP runs IPsec ESP in transport mode
# between the UE and the P-CSCF (TS 33.203), so every SIP message after the
# registration is behind an ESP header and Wireshark leaves all three of these
# off by default. Turned on, a protected Gm frame reads as SIP - packet list,
# dissection tree, flow diagram and ims.lua alike.
#
# The two decode preferences are one mechanism and the heuristic is another; both
# are wanted, because they cover different frames:
#
# - The heuristic needs no keys at all. It only applies when the encryption
#   algorithm really is NULL - which is what this stack negotiates, `ealg=null`
#   in the Security-Server header - and finds the payload by checking that what
#   trails it looks like ESP padding. Left at `esp.padding: rfc`, so a frame that
#   pads any other way is not guessed at.
# - enable_encryption_decode is the keyed path, over the SAs in the esp_sa table:
#   it decrypts (or, for a NULL SA, just trims the trailer) and dissects what
#   comes out. src/esp.go fills that table from the capture, so it covers
#   encrypted Gm as well - see the comment at the top of that file.
#
# enable_authentication_check is the check on all of that: with an SA matched it
# verifies the ICV with the SA's integrity key and says so in the tree, which is
# how a key that came out of the capture wrong is visible instead of silent.
#
# A frame with an SA is dissected by the keyed path and not by the heuristic, so
# the two decode preferences go together: with the check on and the decode off, a
# frame whose SA matches stops at "Data" - the heuristic does not pick it up
# again. Frames with no SA at all are unaffected and stay with the heuristic.
esp.enable_null_encryption_decode_heuristic: TRUE
esp.enable_encryption_decode: TRUE
esp.enable_authentication_check: TRUE
